{%- comment -%}
  PG Share Image: the two things that have to be in the <head>.

  Named for the first of them; it carries the second because it is already
  rendered from layout/theme.liquid's head, and the head is exactly where both
  belong. Splitting them would mean a second render line in the layout for no
  gain.

  ================================================================
  1. THE SEARCH THUMBNAIL (og:image)

  The site emitted NO og:image on any page. With none declared a crawler picks
  an image off the page itself -- Google chose a photo of leg compression boots
  left over from North Cove Wellness, and that became the thumbnail beside the
  search result.

  Declared for the homepage only: product and collection pages get theirs from
  content_for_header and social-meta-tags, and a second declaration would
  compete. Requested at 1200px because Facebook wants 1200 on the long edge and
  Google needs at least that width to consider an image for a rich result.

  ================================================================
  2. THE LOGO FLASH

  Clicking Home, Track Order or Contact showed the old North Cove logo for
  about half a second before it corrected itself.

  pe-logo does the swap with content:url() -- but pe-logo is a SECTION in
  footer-group, and section groups render at the END of the body. The browser
  parses the header, paints whatever <img> the theme settings put there, and
  only later reaches pe-logo's rule and repaints. Warm cache hides it; a cold
  navigation shows the old art for as long as the rest of the page takes to
  arrive.

  CSS in the HEAD is parsed before the header markup exists, so the first paint
  is already correct and there is nothing to repaint.

  The rule is duplicated from pe-logo deliberately. pe-logo still owns the
  glow, the sizing and the JS that rewrites src, and it must keep working on
  its own if this snippet is ever removed. What is here is only the
  substitution -- the minimum needed to make the first paint right.

  asset_url rather than pe-logo's hard-coded CDN path, so a re-uploaded badge
  cannot leave this pointing at a stale version of the file.

  The class list caught every logo the current markup uses, but the old North
  Cove badge is still referenced in the served HTML by a bare <img> that does
  not always carry one of those classes: the file
  768205661_..._n.jpg, rendered with alt="North Cove". Any page that paints
  that <img> before pe-logo's section is reached shows the old art. So the
  swap is matched by SRC and ALT as well as by class -- whichever selector
  hits first, the first paint is the Pocket Era badge. Both attribute matches
  are narrow (the specific North Cove asset id, and the exact North Cove alt),
  so nothing else on the page is touched.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if template.name == 'index' -%}
  {%- assign pg_share = 'https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0815/5706/1860/files/hf_20260812_024500_f32899a3-31ee-4f58-b86d-d1bd7489954e.png' -%}
  <meta property="og:image" content="{{ pg_share }}?width=1200">
  <meta property="og:image:secure_url" content="{{ pg_share }}?width=1200">
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="1200">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Pocket Era glowing crystal orb collectibles">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ pg_share }}?width=1200">
{%- endif -%}
<style>
  /* first paint already correct -- see note 2 above */
  html body .pgh-logo img,
  html body .pgx-logo img,
  html body .pgc-logo img,
  html body .pgf-head img,
  html body img.nc-dlogo,
  html body img.nc-foot-emblem,
  html body img[src*="768205661"],
  html body img[alt="North Cove"]{
    content:url("{{ 'pe-badge.svg' | asset_url }}") !important;
    mix-blend-mode:normal !important;
  }
</style>
{%- comment -%}
  ================================================================
  3. THE DARK-SKIN FIRST PAINT (the white flash on navigation)

  The Pocket Era dark skin lives in two sections, pg-theme-css and pg-dark,
  both rendered from footer-group -- the very END of the body. So the browser
  parses the head (which carries only the theme's LIGHT base CSS), paints the
  page light, and only repaints dark when it finally reaches those two style
  blocks at the bottom. On a cold load, and on every Xtra instant-page
  navigation between tabs, that leaves a visible white flash: a white header
  band at the top and a white footer/newsletter band lower down, for a frame,
  before the dark skin lands.

  The fix is the same one used for the logo above: put the FLASH-CRITICAL
  surface rules in the head, where they are parsed before any body markup
  exists, so the first paint is already dark. This is only the big-surface
  backgrounds (canvas, header, footer) and the text colours that ride on them
  -- the minimum to kill the flash. pg-theme-css and pg-dark still own the full
  detailed skin; when they load at the bottom they simply re-declare these same
  rules and nothing changes. Values and selectors are copied verbatim from
  those two files so the two can never disagree.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /* canvas -- from pg-dark */
  html,body,#content,main,
  body.template-cart,body.template-cart #content,body.template-cart main{
    background:linear-gradient(180deg,#0B0710 0%,#140C1E 52%,#0B0710 100%) !important;
    background-attachment:fixed !important;
    color:#F1EDF7 !important;
  }
  #root{background:transparent !important;background-color:transparent !important}
  #pgh,#pgx,#pgc,.pgm{background:transparent !important}

  /* header / nav surfaces -- from pg-dark */
  #pgx .pgx-head,.pgc-head,.pgf-head,.pgf-nav{
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.90) !important;
    border-bottom:1px solid rgba(168,120,214,.16) !important;
  }
  #content,#pgh,#pgx,#pgc,.pgm{color:#F1EDF7}

  /* footer -- purple, from pg-theme-css (doubled selector keeps its specificity) */
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer,
  .shopify-section[id$="__footer"].shopify-section[id$="__footer"]{
    background:#7A2FA2 !important;background-color:#7A2FA2 !important;
  }
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer h1,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer h2,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer h3,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer h4,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer p,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer li,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer span,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer label,
  #shopify-section-footer#shopify-section-footer div{color:#fff}
</style>
{%- comment -%}
  ================================================================
  4. THE COOKIE BAR (leftover North Cove skin, flashes white)

  The consent bar (#nc-cookie) still wears North Cove's cream-and-teal skin --
  a bright bar that flashes in on every page against the dark storefront.
  Re-skinned here to the Pocket Era palette (near-black surface, lavender text,
  one purple accent on Accept). Head-level so it is dark the instant it shows.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  #nc-cookie,.nc-cookie{
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.97) !important;
    border-top:1px solid rgba(168,120,214,.28) !important;
    box-shadow:0 -14px 40px -24px rgba(0,0,0,.7) !important;
    color:#E4D9F0 !important;
  }
  .nc-cookie-in,.nc-cookie-btns{color:#C1B4D2 !important}
  .nc-cookie-txt{color:#C1B4D2 !important}
  .nc-cookie-txt a{color:#C79BEA !important}
  .nc-cookie-ic{background:rgba(139,68,190,.16) !important}
  .nc-cookie-ic svg{stroke:#C79BEA !important}
  .nc-cookie-decline{
    background:transparent !important;color:#A296B4 !important;
    border:1px solid rgba(168,120,214,.30) !important;
  }
  .nc-cookie-accept{
    background:#8B44BE !important;background-image:none !important;
    color:#fff !important;border:0 !important;
  }
  .nc-cookie-accept:hover{background:#7A2FA2 !important}
</style>
{%- comment -%}
  ================================================================
  5. LOAD CURTAIN -- everything appears together, not in stages

  Killing the white flash made the FIRST frame dark, but a cold load still
  paints in stages: the dark canvas lands first, then the header band, then the
  purple footer, the cookie bar and the product cards each pop in as the browser
  reaches the section-group style blocks at the end of the body, and images fade
  in on top of that. The page assembles itself on screen instead of arriving
  whole.

  This holds a single full-viewport cover -- painted in the EXACT canvas
  gradient, so there is no seam against the page behind it -- over the whole
  document from the very first frame, while all that staged painting happens
  underneath it, and lifts it in one short fade the moment the document is
  parsed (DOMContentLoaded: every section-group <style> block has been read by
  then, so every colour is already in place). The finished page is revealed all
  at once instead of building up piece by piece.

  Safeguards, in order of importance:
   - The cover is opt-IN via a class the inline script adds to <html> before the
     first paint, so with JavaScript off it never appears and the page just
     loads the old way -- it can never trap a no-JS visitor behind a blank
     screen.
   - pointer-events:none, so even mid-hold nothing is ever blocked.
   - A hard 1.5s cap reveals regardless, and the cover element is removed
     outright just after the fade, so nothing can leave it stuck on screen.
   - It keys off a full document parse, so it only affects a real page load; the
     theme's instant-page tab navigation (which swaps the body without
     reparsing) never re-triggers it and is left exactly as it is.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script>
  (function(){
    var h = document.documentElement;
    h.className += ' pg-hold';
    var done = false;
    function reveal(){
      if (done) return; done = true;
      h.classList.add('pg-ready');
      /* drop the cover outright once the fade is done so it can never trap anything */
      setTimeout(function(){ h.classList.remove('pg-hold'); }, 300);
    }
    function schedule(){
      /* two frames: guarantees the page has painted its colours under the cover
         before it lifts, so the reveal shows a finished page, not a blank one */
      requestAnimationFrame(function(){ requestAnimationFrame(reveal); });
    }
    if (document.readyState === 'interactive' || document.readyState === 'complete') schedule();
    else document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', schedule);
    /* hard safety cap -- never hold longer than 1.5s no matter what */
    setTimeout(reveal, 1500);
  })();
</script>
<style>
  html.pg-hold::before{
    content:'';
    position:fixed;
    inset:0;
    z-index:2147483647;
    background:linear-gradient(180deg,#0B0710 0%,#140C1E 52%,#0B0710 100%);
    pointer-events:none;
    opacity:1;
    transition:opacity .2s ease;
  }
  html.pg-ready::before{opacity:0}
</style>
{%- comment -%}
  ================================================================
  6. THE FAVICON (the last piece of North Cove branding on screen)

  The browser tab still showed North Cove's icon. It is not in any template --
  it is a THEME SETTING, config/settings_data.json:

      "favicon": "shopify://shop_images/Gemini_Generated_Image_m5rlmgm5rlmgm5rl.png"

  left behind when this theme was duplicated from the North Cove build, exactly
  like the logo in note 2 and the cookie skin in note 4. layout/theme.liquid
  renders it into a <link rel="icon"> and nothing downstream can change what
  that setting points at.

  So the icon is re-declared here, from assets/pe-badge.svg -- the same Pocket
  Era mark note 2 already swaps the logos to, so the tab and the header cannot
  drift apart. SVG is used deliberately: it is the one favicon format that stays
  sharp at every size a browser asks for, from a 16px tab to a 128px bookmark
  tile, and the badge is vector artwork already.

  WHY THE SCRIPT. Two <link rel="icon"> tags leave the choice to the browser --
  most take the last in document order, but that is convention rather than
  guarantee, and Safari has gone the other way. Rather than rely on it, the
  script deletes every icon link that is not the badge. It runs twice: once
  inline (catching the theme's link when it is emitted ABOVE this snippet) and
  once on DOMContentLoaded (catching it when it is emitted below). Matching is
  on the pe-badge filename, so a future icon of ours survives and only foreign
  ones are removed, and the whole thing is wrapped in try/catch -- a failure
  here leaves the old icon, never a broken head.

  A NOTE ON THE PROPER FIX. The setting itself is still wrong, and this override
  hides that rather than correcting it. Changing it means rewriting the 54KB
  admin-generated settings_data.json wholesale, which the API only accepts as a
  full-file write. The one-click path is Customize -> Theme settings -> Favicon.
  If that is ever done, this block becomes redundant but stays harmless: it
  would simply re-declare the icon the setting already points at.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign pg_icon = 'pe-badge.svg' | asset_url -%}
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ pg_icon }}">
<link rel="shortcut icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ pg_icon }}">
<script>
  (function(){
    function only(){
      try {
        var links = document.querySelectorAll('link[rel~="icon"], link[rel="shortcut icon"], link[rel="apple-touch-icon"], link[rel="apple-touch-icon-precomposed"], link[rel="mask-icon"]');
        for (var i = 0; i < links.length; i++){
          var l = links[i];
          if ((l.getAttribute('href') || '').indexOf('pe-badge') > -1) continue;
          if (l.parentNode) l.parentNode.removeChild(l);
        }
      } catch(e){ /* never break the head */ }
    }
    only();
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', only);
    else only();
  })();
</script>
